  "Question": "Let \\(S=(x-1)^4+4(x-1)^3\\) \\(+6(x-1)^2+4(x-1)+1.\\) Then \\(S\\) equals:",
  "Question Type": "text",
  "Choices": {
    "A": "\\((x-2)^4\\)",
    "B": "\\((x-1)^4\\)",
    "C": "\\(x^4\\)",
    "D": "\\((x+1)^4\\)",
    "E": "\\(x^4+1\\)"
  },
  "Choices Type": "text",
  "Correct Answer": "C",
  "Solution": "Set \\(t=x-1\\), so that all five terms are powers of one quantity. To compare the expression with a fourth power, square \\(t+1\\) twice:\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n(t+1)^4\n&=(t^2+2t+1)^2\\\\\n&=t^4+4t^3+(4+2)t^2+4t+1.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\nThe coefficient \\(6\\) of \\(t^2\\) comes from \\((2t)^2=4t^2\\) and the cross products \\(2(t^2)(1)=2t^2\\). Thus this expansion matches the polynomial defining \\(S\\), giving\n\\[\nS=(t+1)^4=((x-1)+1)^4=x^4.\n\\]\nThe answer is \\(\\mathrm{C}\\).",
